'''McGinley Square''' is a neighborhood in [[Jersey City, New Jersey]], located near the middle of the city, south of [[Journal Square]]. The square itself is at the intersection of two of the city's major thoroughfares, Montgomery Street (which runs from [[Downtown Jersey City|Downtown]] at [[Exchange Place (Jersey City)|Exchange Place]] to [[West Side, Jersey City|West Side]]), and [[Bergen-Lafayette, Jersey City|Bergen Avenue]] (which runs between [[Greenville, Jersey City|Greenville]] and [[Journal Square]]).  
[[File:McGinley Square.jpg|upright=0.6|thumb|left|View of McGinley Square]]

The area was named after Monsignor Roger McGinley, the builder of St. Aedans Church.<ref>Caldes, Charles. "Jersey City's Journal Square". Caldes, 2007. {{ISBN|1-56691-949-5}}</ref> [[Hudson Catholic Regional High School]], the [[Jersey City Armory]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.destinationjerseycity.com/mcginley_square.php|title=McGinley Square|publisher=Destination: Jersey City|archive-date=February 13, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060213203253/http://www.destinationjerseycity.com/mcginley_square.php}}</ref> the [[Jersey City YMCA]], [[St. Mark Coptic Orthodox Church (Jersey City, New Jersey)|St. Mark Coptic Orthodox Church]], and [[Bergen Square]] are all within a short walk from the square.{{citation needed|date=October 2021}}

By the late 2010s, the area around the square was planned for development.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.nj.com/hudson/index.ssf/2011/07/anxious_jersey_city_homeowners.html|title=Anxious Jersey City homeowners told their properties are being taken off eminent domain list|date=18 July 2011}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.weehawkenreporter.com/view/full_story/14791592/article-McGinley-East-residents-condemn-plan-Eminent-domain-is-off-the-table-%E2%80%93-but-for-how-long---?instance=secondary_stories_left_column |title=Hudson Reporter - McGinley East residents condemn plan Eminent domain is off the table – but for how long |access-date=2011-07-24 |archive-date=2011-09-29 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110929114245/http://www.weehawkenreporter.com/view/full_story/14791592/article-McGinley-East-residents-condemn-plan-Eminent-domain-is-off-the-table-%E2%80%93-but-for-how-long---?instance=secondary_stories_left_column |url-status=dead }}</ref> and was chosen by the [[U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development]] to receive a $250,000 Choice Neighborhoods grant to explore ways to develop the area into a mixed-use neighborhood. Residents formed the McGinley Square Redevelopment Plan, which called for the construction of affordable and workforce housing, retail outlets, restaurants, and entertainment facilities.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.njfuture.org/smart-growth-101/stories/2012-smart-growth-award-winners/2012-sga-mcginley/|title=A University-Neighborhood Revitalization Partnership in Jersey City &#124; New Jersey Future|date=28 April 2012}}</ref> Large blocks of the neighborhood belong to [[Saint Peter's College (New Jersey)|Saint Peter's University]], which in March 2016 received approval to build a 21-story mixed use tower at 688-700 Montgomery Street,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://jerseydigs.com/saint-peters-tower-hopes-to-transform-mcginley-square/|title=Saint Peter's Tower Hopes to Transform McGinley Square|publisher=[[Jersey Digs]]|date=March 11, 2016|access-date=October 18, 2021|archive-date=March 18, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160318185132/https://jerseydigs.com/saint-peters-tower-hopes-to-transform-mcginley-square/}}</ref> which included three levels of retail and entertainment space, including a movie theater and 300-car parking garage.<ref>{{Citation   | last = Martin|first = Antoinette| title = Revitalizing a Neighborhood in Jersey City| newspaper =[[The New York Times]]| date = April 2011   | url = https://www.nytimes.com/2011/04/10/realestate/10njzo.html?_r=1&scp=16&sq=west%20side%20jersey%20city&st=csehttp://www.nj.com/hudson/index.ssf/2011/06/jersey_city_paying_consultant.html| access-date =2011-06-18}}</ref> Plans were mired in a controversy regarding the use of [[eminent domain]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://hudsonreporter.com/view/full_stories_home/15071472/article-McGinley-Square-East-plan-stalls-City-to-reconsider-controversial-redevelopment-?instance=jersey_city_story_left_column|title=McGinley Square East plan stalls: City to reconsider controversial redevelopment|author=Wright, E. Assata|newspaper=[[The Hudson Reporter]]|date=August 14, 2011|archive-date=August 14, 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110814182734/https://hudsonreporter.com/view/full_stories_home/15071472/article-McGinley-Square-East-plan-stalls-City-to-reconsider-controversial-redevelopment-?instance=jersey_city_story_left_column}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.jerseycityindependent.com/2011/09/09/jersey-city-completely-pulls-eminent-domain-from-mcginley-square-east-redevelopment-plan/ |title=Jersey City Completely Pulls Eminent Domain from McGinley Square East Redevelopment Plan &#124; the Jersey City Independent |access-date=September 9, 2011 |archive-date=March 17, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120317021848/http://www.jerseycityindependent.com/2011/09/09/jersey-city-completely-pulls-eminent-domain-from-mcginley-square-east-redevelopment-plan |url-status=dead }}</ref> but were nonetheless approved. Construction had been set to begin in August 2012, with completion set for 2016;<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.jerseycityindependent.com/2011/09/28/city-council-pushes-mcginley-square-east-redevelopment-plan-forward/ |title=City Council Pushes McGinley Square East Redevelopment Plan Forward &#124; the Jersey City Independent |access-date=September 30, 2011 |archive-date=March 17, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120317011639/http://www.jerseycityindependent.com/2011/09/28/city-council-pushes-mcginley-square-east-redevelopment-plan-forward |url-status=dead }}</ref> as of 2018 it had not yet begun.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://jerseydigs.com/construction-yet-begin-spus-21-story-tower-mcginley-square/|title = No Construction Yet for Saint Peter's University Tower in McGinley Square|date = 15 November 2017}}</ref>

Several community organizations are active in the McGinley Square area. Bergen Communities United is a non-profit organization responsible for community planning in McGinley Square and the neighborhoods immediately to the south. The McGinley Square Partnership is a [[business improvement district]], with local business owners using dues and NJ [[Urban Enterprise Zone]] funds for improvements to the commercial area. The Highland Avenue Block Association advocates for residents' interests.{{citation needed|date=April 2021}}

